---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import EditorThemePicker from "../components/EditorThemePicker.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Oreslang Philosophy — explicit syntax, safer runtimes"
  description="Why Oreslang chooses explicit syntax, actor isolation, borrowing, option types, hybrid GC, non-cooperative scheduling, hot code loading, and a path toward location-transparent distributed actors."
>
  <section class="page-hero philosophy-hero">
    <p class="kicker">Philosophy</p>
    <h1>Spend syntax to buy certainty.</h1>
    <p>
      In the age of AI-assisted programming, terseness is less sacred. Oreslang is more explicit
      when those extra tokens reduce ambiguity and make static analysis, AOT compilation, and
      JIT specialization faster.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="page-body">
    <div class="prose">
      <div class="terminal-trace code-editor" data-code-editor data-code-key="philosophy-runtime-trace" data-editor-theme="vscode" aria-label="Illustrative Oreslang runtime stack trace">
        <div class="terminal-bar">
          <span class="terminal-dot"></span>
          <span class="terminal-dot"></span>
          <span class="terminal-dot"></span>
          <strong>ores · runtime</strong>
          <EditorThemePicker label="philosophy runtime trace" />
        </div>
        <pre><code><span class="term-prompt">$</span> ores run app.ores --mode=hybrid
<span class="term-dim">[oresvm]</span> loaded code image app.aot <span class="term-tag">AOT</span>
<span class="term-dim">[oresvm]</span> Worker.run crossed hot threshold <span class="term-tag">JIT tier 2</span>

<span class="term-error">OresError[E_OPTION_NONE]</span>: unwrap() called on Option.none
  --> src/worker.ores:18:14
  18 | const job = next.unwrap();
     |                  ^^^^^^

stack trace:
  0: Worker.run(Job)          src/worker.ores:18
  1: QueueActor.dispatch(Job) src/queue.ores:44
  2: actor#worker-17.mailbox  &lt;oresvm&gt;

runtime frames:
  code image   app.aot       <span class="term-tag">AOT</span>
  hot frame    Worker.run    <span class="term-tag">JIT tier 2</span>
  actor        worker-17     <span class="term-tag">isolated</span></code></pre>
      </div>

      <h3>AOT + JIT are complementary</h3>
      <p>
        Oreslang is designed so one static language surface can support two compilation strategies
        without forcing applications into two different programming models. Ahead-of-time compilation
        can turn verified code and runtime metadata into deployable images with predictable startup,
        while JIT compilation can specialize the hot functions, actor routes, and data shapes that only
        become obvious after a program is running.
      </p>

      <div class="compile-grid">
        <article class="compile-card">
          <span>AOT</span>
          <h4>Compile before deployment</h4>
          <p>Resolve types, ownership rules, closed shapes, imports, and as much dispatch as possible before the program starts.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="compile-card">
          <span>JIT</span>
          <h4>Specialize what gets hot</h4>
          <p>Use runtime profiles to optimize frequently executed actor handlers and routines without weakening the static contract.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="compile-card">
          <span>IR</span>
          <h4>Share the same semantic core</h4>
          <p>A stable typed IR can feed Graal/Truffle today and later native backends such as LLVM without redesigning the language.</p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <p>
        Extra explicitness is useful here: fewer ambiguous constructs mean less compiler work proving
        what a program means, more stable AOT output, and cleaner specialization opportunities for a JIT.
      </p>

      <h3>Memory safety should be layered</h3>
      <p>
        The target is Rust-class memory safety without requiring every workload to live at Rust's
        level of manual lifetime ceremony. Oreslang combines static types, a borrow checker,
        immutable-by-default values, option types instead of null, actor isolation, and runtime GC.
        The compiler should prevent invalid ownership and lifetime states before GC ever sees them;
        the runtime collector remains available for managed graphs and cycles.
      </p>
      <p>
        GC is therefore not treated as an excuse to ignore ownership. Nor is ownership treated as
        a reason the runtime can never collect. The model is deliberately hybrid: compile-time
        lifetime information first, automatic collection where useful, plus explicit lifetime/GC
        controls for latency-sensitive runtimes.
      </p>

      <h3>Actors are a memory-safety primitive</h3>
      <p>
        Oreslang actors are intended to make the safest concurrency model the easiest one. Shared
        actors can cooperate inside one process where sharing is allowed. Isolated and untrusted
        actors get confined memory regions for mutable state, restricted capabilities, serialized mailbox boundaries where
        required, and stronger rules about what code and memory can cross the boundary.
      </p>
      <p>
        That matters on FaaS hosts and on Android/iOS, where the runtime may need dense in-process
        isolation but cannot assume that every workload cooperates with a single event loop.
        Oreslang therefore treats non-cooperative scheduling, cancellation, quotas, and actor
        confinement as runtime concerns rather than application conventions.
      </p>

      <div class="principle-grid">
        <article class="principle">
          <span>01</span>
          <h4>Static by default</h4>
          <p>Static types, closed shapes, option types, exhaustive matching, and compile-time sendability rules.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="principle">
          <span>02</span>
          <h4>Borrow + collect</h4>
          <p>Borrow checking narrows lifetime mistakes; GC handles managed object graphs instead of carrying the whole safety burden.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="principle">
          <span>03</span>
          <h4>Make effects visible</h4>
          <p><code>pure</code> and <code>trap</code> make logic safety and error boundaries explicit enough for humans and compilers to reason about.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="principle">
          <span>04</span>
          <h4>Expose lexical intent</h4>
          <p><code>alex</code> / <code>nlex</code> modes are designed to expose capture and lexical-scope intent for stronger compiler optimization.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="principle">
          <span>05</span>
          <h4>Schedule hostile work</h4>
          <p>Actors can be placed in cooperative or non-cooperative execution pools instead of assuming every task yields politely.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="principle">
          <span>06</span>
          <h4>Accelerators are language domains</h4>
          <p>GPU and quantum/QNTM routines are part of the language design, so accelerator placement can be explicit rather than hidden behind ad-hoc FFI.</p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <h3>Hot code loading without surrendering trust boundaries</h3>
      <p>
        Trusted and untrusted code should both be reloadable, but not under the same rules.
        Compatible immutable code images can be shared across actors inside one OresVM instead of
        being copied per actor. Untrusted actors additionally require whitelisting and capability
        checks before code or host functionality becomes visible.
      </p>

      <div class="future-card">
        <p class="kicker">Future runtime direction</p>
        <h3>One actor model, more than one machine.</h3>
        <p>
          Actor isolation already gives Oreslang a natural seam for distribution. The long-term
          abstraction should preserve the same typed actor reference while the runtime chooses
          transport and placement: local shared memory, isolated memory, IPC, TCP, or an external
          host. Placement affinity can prefer the same machine, require a different machine, or
          delegate to an external scheduler without rewriting actor business logic.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is intentionally a runtime direction, not finalized surface syntax. The goal is to
          avoid creating a separate language that suddenly appears when an actor moves across a
          process or network boundary. Remote mailboxes would become serialized transport, making
          the model start to resemble systems such as Vert.x while preserving Oreslang's actor
          safety rules.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3>How to build your own language</h3>
      <ol class="language-steps">
        <li><strong>Start with Truffle on the JVM.</strong> Get parsing, semantics, instrumentation, and an executable language model working quickly.</li>
        <li><strong>Use GraalVM for JIT and AOT experiments.</strong> A language on Truffle is not limited to interpretation or JIT-only execution.</li>
        <li><strong>Stabilize syntax after proving compilation.</strong> Once the language surface feels right, prove representative programs can compile ahead of time with the runtime constraints you care about.</li>
        <li><strong>Then lower through an IR.</strong> LLVM and other native backends become much easier to approach once the language semantics and intermediate representation are no longer moving every day.</li>
      </ol>

      <p class="status-note">
        Some items on this page describe design goals and staged runtime work rather than claiming
        every backend is production-complete today. The compiler and VM are being built in public.
      </p>

      <p><a href="/#philosophy">← Back to the Philosophy section on the home page</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
